---
import Architecture from '../../layouts/Architecture.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/arch/Diagram.astro';
import Box from '../../components/arch/Box.astro';
import Edge from '../../components/arch/Edge.astro';
import Group from '../../components/arch/Group.astro';
import Label from '../../components/arch/Label.astro';
import Cell from '../../components/arch/Cell.astro';
import Legend from '../../components/arch/Legend.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/arch/Callout.astro';

const rows = [
  { cell: '-2', kind: 'buffer', slot: 'bufferStart[1]' },
  { cell: '-1', kind: 'buffer', slot: 'bufferStart[0]' },
  { cell: '0', kind: 'plain', slot: 'visible[0]' },
  { cell: '1', kind: 'plain', slot: 'visible[1]' },
  { cell: '2', kind: 'plain', slot: 'visible[2]' },
  { cell: '3', kind: 'buffer', slot: 'bufferEnd[0]' },
  { cell: '4', kind: 'buffer', slot: 'bufferEnd[1]' },
] as const;
// Cells of 48 on a 56 pitch, starting at y = 60.
const ry = (i: number) => 60 + i * 56;

const layers = [
  ['base weights', 'symbolData[id].weight'],
  ['global spinning', "set(pool) or { slots: 'spinning' }"],
  ['per-reel spinning', '{ reel: i }'],
  ['global buffer', "{ slots: 'buffer' }"],
  ['per-reel buffer', "{ reel: i, slots: 'buffer' }"],
  ['global side', "{ slots: 'bufferStart' | 'bufferEnd' }"],
  ['per-reel side', "{ reel: i, slots: 'bufferStart' | ... }"],
];

const helpers = [
  ['getTargetSlot(target, cell)', 'Reads one slot by cell coordinate. A negative cell reads bufferStart[-1 - cell]; a cell at or past visible.length reads bufferEnd[cell - visible.length]; anything not set comes back undefined.'],
  ['setTargetSlot(target, cell, id)', 'Writes the same coordinate, creating or extending the buffer arrays as needed. It mutates the target, so take cloneColumnTarget(target) first if you still need the original; the clone is one level deep. Writing undefined (the internal clearTargetSlot) means "random-fill this slot".'],
  ['columnTargetToStrip(target, bufferStart)', 'Produces the strip form from the figure: strip[i] = getTargetSlot(target, i - bufferStart).'],
  ['assertColumnTargets(grid, label)', 'Throws a readable TypeError when handed the pre-v2 string[][] shape, with the fix in the message: wrap each column as { visible }.'],
  ['assertBufferCountsInRange(grid, bufferStartPerReel, bufferEndPerReel, label)', 'Throws a RangeError when the highest defined buffer index is at or past the reel\'s buffer capacity. Sparse arrays are fine, and an empty buffer is always in range.'],
];

const mutations = [
  ['set(pool | null, scope)', 'Checks every id is registered, stores a copy, clears the cached tables, then compiles every scope that could be drawn from. If any scope would have nothing left to draw it rolls back and throws "No symbol left to draw for ... cells on reel N", so a bad pool never reaches a spin.'],
  ['setExcludeSpinning(ids), setExcludeBuffer(ids)', 'Sugar over set() that keeps the existing weights on the global pool and only changes the exclusions.'],
  ['updateWeights(symbolsData)', 'Re-reads the base weights, for a game-mode switch, and prunes any pool entry that names an id no longer present, so stale exclusions do not linger.'],
];
---
<Architecture
  title="The frame pipeline"
  subtitle="How a result grid becomes the symbols that land, wrap by wrap."
  eyebrow="Identity"
>
  <p>
    The server tells your game how a spin ends: "reel one shows ace, king,
    ten". The reel, meanwhile, is a strip of symbols that is longer than the
    window, scrolling past at thirty cells a second and refilling itself
    from the top. Somewhere between those two facts, every symbol the
    server named has to be slipped into the strip at exactly the right
    moment so that when the reel snaps to the grid, those three are the
    ones in the window, the half-visible symbols above and below them look
    plausible, and nothing the player saw a moment ago has changed under
    their eyes.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <a href="/architecture/motion/">motion page</a> explains <em>where</em> every symbol is at any instant. This page is about <em>which</em> symbol sits in each seat. The answer has three parts: the
    shape the result arrives in, one column at a time; the small pipeline
    that turns a column into a complete list of ids for the strip, buffers
    included; and the random provider that fills in everything the server
    did not specify. All of it lives under <code>packages/pixi-reels/src/frame/</code>.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="plain">
    A result is one target per column: the visible symbols, and optionally
    a few for the buffer cells beyond the window. The engine expands each
    target into a full strip of ids, filling the gaps randomly, and hands
    the strip to the reel. As the reel spins out, it takes those ids one
    per wrap, and when the last one has entered, the strip is the result.
  </Callout>

  <h2>ColumnTarget: one column, visible-relative</h2>

  <p>
    A <strong>column target</strong> describes what one reel should end up
    showing. It is a plain object with a <code>visible</code> array (the
    symbols in the window, top to bottom) and two optional arrays for the
    buffer cells, <code>bufferStart</code> for the side with the smaller
    coordinate (above, on a vertical set) and <code>bufferEnd</code> for the
    other side. The buffer arrays count outward from the window, so <code>bufferStart[0]</code> is the cell just above the first visible one.
    Most games only ever fill in <code>visible</code>; the buffers exist for
    the cases where what peeks out of the window matters, like a big symbol
    whose tail should be visible before the whole thing has landed.
  </p>

  <p>
    Figure 1 shows one target addressed three ways, with two buffer cells
    per side. Read the columns left to right: the strip index, which starts
    at the furthest buffer above; the <strong>cell coordinate</strong>, where
    0 is the first visible cell and buffers above are negative; and the
    field of the target each one maps to. Notice that the cell coordinate
    is the one the public API uses everywhere, because it does not change
    when you change the buffer size.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={510} caption="One ColumnTarget addressed three ways, with bufferStart = 2: by strip index (0 is the furthest buffer-above slot), by cell coordinate (0 is the first visible cell, the dashed outline is the window), and by field.">
    <Label x={90} y={42} text="strip[i]" anchor="end" weight={600} tone="fg" />
    <Label x={144} y={42} text="cell" anchor="middle" weight={600} tone="fg" />
    <Label x={200} y={42} text="ColumnTarget field" weight={600} tone="fg" />
    <Label x={400} y={42} text="getTargetSlot(target, cell)" weight={600} tone="fg" />
    {rows.map((r, i) => (
      <>
        <Label x={90} y={ry(i) + 29} text={`${i}`} anchor="end" mono tone="fg" />
        <Cell x={120} y={ry(i)} size={48} label={r.cell} kind={r.kind} />
        <Label x={200} y={ry(i) + 29} text={r.slot} mono tone="fg" />
      </>
    ))}
    <rect x={114} y={ry(2) - 6} width={60} height={3 * 56 - 8 + 12} rx="8" class="guide-strong" style="stroke-dasharray: 4 3" />
    <Label x={400} y={ry(0) + 57} text="bufferStart[-1 - cell]" mono />
    <Label x={400} y={ry(3) + 29} text="visible[cell]" mono />
    <Label x={400} y={ry(5) + 57} text="bufferEnd[cell - visible.length]" mono />
    <Label x={360} y={492} text="strip[i] = getTargetSlot(target, i - bufferStart)" size={14} mono tone="fg" anchor="middle" />
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    The formula at the bottom of the figure converts between the two
    numberings. A strip index counts from the top of the whole array, a
    cell coordinate counts from the top of the window, and the window
    starts <code>bufferStart</code> seats down the array, so subtracting <code>bufferStart</code> from a strip index gives the cell coordinate.
    The strip itself is <code>bufferStart + visible.length + bufferEnd.length</code> ids long. Any slot the target does not name comes back <code>undefined</code>, and the engine fills it with a random symbol. A
    target entry that lies beyond the reel's buffer capacity would be
    silently dropped by the conversion, which is why every public entry
    point runs <code>assertBufferCountsInRange</code> first and refuses it
    with an error instead.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-8">
    <Legend items={[
      { kind: 'muted', label: 'buffer slot', note: 'bufferStart[0] is nearest the window top, bufferEnd[0] nearest the bottom' },
      { kind: 'subtle', label: 'visible slot', note: 'visible[0..visibleCells-1]' },
      { kind: 'primary', label: 'FrameBuilder', note: 'produces the strip' },
      { kind: 'accent', label: 'entry points', note: 'setResult, initialFrame, refill' },
    ]} />
  </div>

  <p>
    The exact type, in <code>frame/ColumnTarget.ts</code>, is <code>{`{ visible: string[]; bufferStart?: (string | undefined)[]; bufferEnd?: (string | undefined)[] }`}</code>.
    It is deliberately plain data with no methods, so it survives <code>structuredClone</code>, JSON and <code>postMessage</code>, and a
    worker or a server can produce it directly. A handful of exported
    helpers read and write it; the table lists them with what each is for.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-4 rounded-xl border border-border bg-card overflow-hidden">
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead class="bg-muted/60">
        <tr>
          <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">Helper</th>
          <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">What it does</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {helpers.map(([k, v]) => (
          <tr class="border-t border-border/60">
            <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-xs align-top">{k}</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-xs text-foreground/80">{v}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <Callout kind="gotcha">
    Pass columns as <code>{`{ visible: [...] }`}</code> objects, never as bare
    arrays. The old <code>string[][]</code> shape used to fail deep inside the
    pipeline after the reels were already moving, and the spin promise never
    settled: a reel that spins forever with no clue why. A recipe on this
    site shipped exactly that bug, which is why <code>assertColumnTargets</code> now catches it at the door with a
    message that shows the fix.
  </Callout>

  <p>
    Big symbols use the same shape with one convention. The server sends
    the id at the block's <strong>anchor</strong> cell only, and <code>coordinateBigSymbols</code> (<code>spin/bigSymbolBlocks.ts</code>)
    checks that each block fits and then paints a sentinel id, <code>OCCUPIED_SENTINEL</code> (<code>'__pixi_reels_occupied__'</code>),
    into every other cell the block covers, across neighbouring reels too.
    An anchor may sit in <code>bufferStart</code> with its tail reaching into
    the window, the "tail-visible" partial landing, and a block anchored on
    the last visible cell spills its stubs into <code>bufferEnd</code>. The <a href="/guides/buffer-indexing/">buffer indexing guide</a> has the
    worked cases.
  </p>

  <h2>The middleware chain</h2>

  <p>
    Turning a target into a strip is a two-step job: fill every slot with
    something plausible, then overwrite the slots the target actually
    names. The <code>FrameBuilder</code> does it as a small <strong>middleware chain</strong>, the same pattern as an HTTP server:
    each middleware receives a context object and a <code>next()</code> function, does its part, and calls <code>next()</code> to pass control
    on. Figure 2 shows the two built-in middlewares and where one of yours
    would sit. Notice that the chain is ordered by a priority number, and
    that the built-ins leave room on either side and in between.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={520} caption="FrameBuilder.build runs a next()-style chain sorted by ascending priority. The two built-ins fill every slot and then overwrite the defined target slots; yours goes before, between or after them by priority.">
    <Box x={180} y={20} w={360} h={78} kind="code" title="FrameContext" sub={["symbols: '' x M, target?, metadata", 'reelIndex, visibleCells, buffers']} />
    <Box x={180} y={124} w={360} h={78} title="random-fill" sub={['priority 0: every slot drawn by kind', "'bufferStart', 'spinning', 'bufferEnd'"]} />
    <Box x={180} y={228} w={360} h={78} title="target-placement" sub={['priority 10: columnTargetToStrip()', 'overwrites every defined id']} />
    <Box x={180} y={332} w={360} h={78} kind="ghost" title="your middleware" sub={['any priority; sort is stable', 'no next() stops the chain here']} />
    <Box x={180} y={436} w={360} h={60} kind="primary" title="string[] strip" sub="index 0 = the furthest buffer-above slot" />
    <Edge from={[360, 98]} to={[360, 124]} />
    <Edge from={[360, 202]} to={[360, 228]} />
    <Edge from={[360, 306]} to={[360, 332]} />
    <Edge from={[360, 410]} to={[360, 436]} />
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    The context, <code>FrameContext</code>, is what the middlewares work on.
    It carries <code>symbols</code>, an array of <code>M</code> empty strings
    to fill; the optional <code>target</code>; a <code>metadata</code> object
    that is fresh for every build, for passing notes between middlewares;
    and the reel's geometry: <code>reelIndex</code>, <code>visibleCells</code>, <code>bufferStart</code>, <code>bufferEnd</code> and <code>isSpinning</code>.
    The first built-in, <strong>random-fill</strong> at priority 0, draws an
    id for every slot. It knows which kind of slot it is filling, buffer
    above, visible, or buffer below, and draws from the matching pool, so a
    collectible that should never be half-visible can be excluded from the
    buffers alone. The second, <strong>target-placement</strong> at priority
    10, converts the target to strip form and overwrites every slot where
    the target has an id.
  </p>

  <p>
    Where you put your own middleware changes what it sees. Above priority
    10 it sees the finished frame and can inspect or adjust it. Below 0 it
    runs first and can pre-seed slots; the random fill keeps what it wrote,
    because the fill only draws into empty strings. Between 0 and 10 it sees
    the random ids but will be overwritten wherever the target has an id.
    The sort is lazy and stable, so two middlewares with equal priority keep
    the order they were registered in. Register with <code>builder.frameMiddleware(mw)</code>; <code>FrameBuilder.use(mw)</code>, <code>remove(name)</code> and the <code>middleware</code> getter also
    exist. There is no separate <code>FrameAPI</code> type: <code>FrameContext</code> plus <code>FrameMiddleware {`{ name, priority, process(ctx, next) }`}</code> is
    the whole surface. A middleware that does not call <code>next()</code> stops the chain there.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="why">
    Random-fill draws one id for every slot, even the ones target-placement
    overwrites a moment later. That looks wasteful, and it is the point: with
    a seeded random number generator, and no middleware pre-seeding slots,
    the number of draws per build is always <code>M</code> per reel whatever
    the result looks like, so a replay from the same seed produces the same
    strip. The OCCUPIED sentinels for big
    symbols arrive through the target and win by overwrite in the same way.
    A comment in <code>SpinController</code> says random fill "skips them";
    by ordering it is the overwrite that wins, and the code is the source of
    truth here.
  </Callout>

  <p>
    <code>buildAll(reelCount, ...)</code> maps <code>build</code> over the
    reels. The <code>randomProvider</code> getter is <code>@internal</code>: a
    middleware that needs a random id should read the slot it is filling, or
    carry its own provider, rather than reach into the builder's.
  </p>

  <h2>When it runs</h2>

  <p>
    It is easy to assume the builder runs every time a symbol wraps. It does
    not. Figure 3 shows the three callers, and each runs it once per reel
    per result: the builder's initial frame, the spin result, and a cascade
    refill. Notice that the spin result does not reach the builder straight
    away; it waits at a gate until every reel is actually in its spin phase,
    and passes through the big-symbol coordinator first.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={400} caption="Who calls build(). FrameBuilder is not called per wrap: it runs once per reel per result, from three callers, and the spin result passes the gate and the big-symbol coordinator first.">
    <Box x={16} y={20} w={210} h={78} title="ReelSetBuilder.build()" sub={['the initial frame,', 'seeded by .initialFrame()']} />
    <Box x={16} y={124} w={210} h={78} kind="accent" title="setResult(grid)" sub={['asserts the grid, overlays', 'pins, then _resultSymbols']} />
    <Box x={16} y={228} w={210} h={78} title="refill({ grid })" sub={['cascade: frames built', 'up front from the grid']} />

    <Box x={256} y={124} w={190} h={78} title="gate" sub={["every reel in 'spin'", 'then begin the stop']} />
    <Box x={476} y={124} w={228} h={78} title="coordinateBigSymbols" sub={['validates block fit,', 'paints OCCUPIED (pure)']} />
    <Edge from={[226, 163]} to={[256, 163]} />
    <Edge from={[446, 163]} to={[476, 163]} />

    <Box x={256} y={310} w={448} h={78} kind="primary" title="FrameBuilder.build(...)" sub={['(reelIndex, cells, bufferStart, bufferEnd, target)', 'once per reel per result']} />
    <Edge from={[590, 202]} to={[590, 310]} />
    <Edge from={[226, 59]} via={[[241, 59], [241, 349]]} to={[256, 349]} />
    <Edge from={[226, 267]} via={[[241, 267], [241, 349]]} to={[256, 349]} />
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    Once a strip exists it has to reach the reel at the right moment, which
    is not when the result arrives but when that reel begins to stop.
    Figure 4 follows one strip from the builder to the wrap callback. Notice
    the cache in the middle: the strip is built as soon as the result is
    accepted, parked per reel, and only read when the stop phase for that
    reel starts, which may be seconds later for the last reel.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={560} caption="From the cached strip to the sequencer. The strip is cached per reel, read at stop time, loaded into the StopSequencer with the reel's feed edge, and consumed one id per wrap.">
    <Box x={180} y={20} w={360} h={60} kind="primary" title="FrameBuilder.build(...)" sub="one string[] per reel" />
    <Box x={180} y={104} w={360} h={60} title="_cachedFrames[i]" sub="held reels get []" />
    <Box x={180} y={188} w={360} h={60} title="StopPhase.targetFrame" sub="_frameFor(i), at stop time" />
    <Box x={180} y={272} w={360} h={60} title="Reel.setStopFrame(frame)" sub="the spinOut step" />
    <Box x={180} y={356} w={360} h={78} kind="accent" title="StopSequencer.setFrame()" sub={['forward: end-first, cursor M-1', 'reverse: head-first, cursor 0']} />
    <Box x={180} y={458} w={360} h={60} title="Reel._onSymbolWrapped()" sub="next() once per wrap while stopping" />
    <Edge from={[360, 80]} to={[360, 104]} />
    <Edge from={[360, 164]} to={[360, 188]} />
    <Edge from={[360, 248]} to={[360, 272]} />
    <Edge from={[360, 332]} to={[360, 356]} />
    <Edge from={[360, 434]} to={[360, 458]} />
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    The three callers in detail. The builder makes the initial frame when
    you call <code>build()</code>, seeded by whatever you passed to <code>.initialFrame()</code>. The spin result is built once every reel
    that is not held has entered its spin phase, in <code>_tryBeginStopSequence</code> in <code>packages/pixi-reels/src/spin/SpinController.ts</code>; on a
    MultiWays set it uses the pending shape for the per-reel cell count, so
    a reshape lands the right number of symbols. A cascade refill builds all
    its frames up front from the grid you pass. In every case the output is
    a <code>string[]</code>, the same format <code>Reel.placeStrip</code> accepts when a skip, a turbo stop or a cascade places a whole strip at
    once; <code>placeStrip</code> random-fills any <code>undefined</code> entry by slot kind. The last box in figure 4 is the join with the motion
    page: while a reel is stopping, each wrap consumes one id from the
    sequencer, and every other wrap draws from the provider directly. The
    gate and the phases around it are on the <a href="/architecture/spin-lifecycle/">spin lifecycle page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>RandomSymbolProvider: layered pools</h2>

  <p>
    Everything the server did not specify, which is most of what scrolls
    past during a spin, comes from the <code>RandomSymbolProvider</code>.
    At its simplest it is a weighted draw: every registered symbol has a
    weight, and a heavier symbol appears more often. On top of that base
    you can layer <strong>pools</strong>, each of which adjusts weights or
    excludes ids for one scope: all reels or one reel, and the spinning
    slots or the buffer slots. Figure 5 shows the layers in the order they
    are applied, widest first, so that a narrower scope always wins over a
    wider one. Notice that a draw for a visible slot stops after three
    layers, while a draw for a buffer slot runs through all seven.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={580} caption="One draw resolves the base weights through up to six pool layers, widest first. A 'spinning' draw stops after the third layer; a 'bufferStart' or 'bufferEnd' draw runs all seven, the 'buffer' layers being what both sides inherit.">
    {layers.map(([t, s], i) => (
      <Box x={16} y={20 + i * 80} w={380} h={60} title={t} sub={s} mono={false} kind={i === 0 ? 'primary' : i % 2 === 1 ? 'subtle' : 'muted'} />
    ))}
    {[0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5].map((i) => (
      <Edge from={[206, 80 + i * 80]} to={[206, 100 + i * 80]} />
    ))}
    <Label x={420} y={214} text="'spinning' draws stop here" />
    <Label x={420} y={374} text="'buffer': both sides inherit" />
    <Label x={420} y={534} text="side draws run all seven" />
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    Resolving the layers on every draw would be slow, so the provider
    compiles each scope into a table once and caches it. Figure 6 shows the
    compile and the draw. A weight set by a later layer replaces the
    earlier one for that id; an exclusion can only ever remove an id, never
    bring it back; the result is a list of ids with cumulative weights, and
    a draw is one random number and a binary search.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={180} caption="The compiled table. Weights override per id, exclusions only accumulate, and the result is a cumulative table that next() binary-searches with one rng() call.">
    <Box x={12} y={30} w={216} h={78} kind="code" title="_resolve(slots, reel)" sub={['layer weights override ids', 'excluded ids end at 0']} />
    <Box x={252} y={30} w={216} h={78} title="DrawTable" sub={['ids, cumulative, total', 'cached per slots:reel']} />
    <Box x={492} y={30} w={216} h={78} kind="primary" title="next(slot, reel)" sub={['rand = rng() * total', 'first cumulative > rand']} />
    <Edge from={[228, 69]} to={[252, 69]} />
    <Edge from={[468, 69]} to={[492, 69]} />
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    The provider (<code>frame/RandomSymbolProvider.ts</code>, internal,
    implementing <code>RandomSymbolControl</code>) is built from the symbol
    data and a random function that defaults to <code>Math.random</code>.
    Regulated or provably fair deployments inject a seeded generator with <code>builder.rng(fn)</code>; the cheats package ships a Mulberry32 for
    tests. The constructor throws if there are no symbols or the total weight
    is not positive. A draw names one of three slot kinds, <code>'spinning'</code>, <code>'bufferStart'</code> or <code>'bufferEnd'</code>; <code>'buffer'</code> is a layer you configure
    that both sides inherit, never a kind you draw from. Tables are cached
    under <code>{"`${slots}:${reel ?? '*'}`"}</code> with zero-weight ids
    dropped, and the whole cache is cleared on any change to the pools.
    The table lists the ways the pools can change.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-4 rounded-xl border border-border bg-card overflow-hidden">
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead class="bg-muted/60">
        <tr>
          <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">Mutation</th>
          <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">What it does</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {mutations.map(([k, v]) => (
          <tr class="border-t border-border/60">
            <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-xs align-top">{k}</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-xs text-foreground/80">{v}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Four places draw from it. A wrap on a spinning reel draws <code>'spinning'</code> (<code>Reel._onSymbolWrapped</code>). A buffer
    refill in <code>placeStrip</code> and the padding for a nudge draw <code>'bufferStart'</code> or <code>'bufferEnd'</code>. And a pin
    backfill draws through the <code>ReelSet</code>.
  </p>

  <h3>Pool scopes and the public control</h3>

  <p>
    A pool, <code>SymbolPool</code> in <code>frame/SymbolPool.ts</code>, is <code>{`{ weights?: Record<id, number>; exclude?: string[] }`}</code>; a
    weight of 0 removes an id as completely as excluding it. Its scope, <code>SymbolPoolScope</code>, is <code>{`{ reel?: number; slots?: SymbolPoolSlots }`}</code>,
    where the slots are <code>'spinning'</code>, <code>'buffer'</code>, <code>'bufferStart'</code> or <code>'bufferEnd'</code> and default to <code>'spinning'</code>. <code>'bufferStart'</code> is always the side
    with the smaller coordinate, above on a vertical set and left on a
    horizontal one, whichever way the reel travels. The typical buffer-only
    pool is <code>{`{ exclude: ['COIN'] }`}</code>, so a collectible never
    peeks in half-visible and raises a hope the result will not pay. These
    random-draw pools are a different thing from the object pools on the <a href="/architecture/symbols/">symbols page</a>, which recycle symbol
    instances.
  </p>

  <p>
    At runtime you reach the pools through <code>reelSet.randomSymbols</code>,
    the <code>RandomSymbolControl</code>: <code>set(pool | null, scope?)</code> replaces the pool for one scope, <code>clear()</code> drops them all, and <code>weights(scope?)</code> reports the effective weights with excluded
    ids shown as 0 (<code>'buffer'</code> reports what both sides inherit).
    At build time the same pools come from <code>builder.randomSymbols(pool, scope)</code>.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="gotcha">
    A pool change applies to the <em>next draw</em>, not to the strip. The
    symbols already on a reel stay until they wrap out, so if you change
    pools after <code>spin()</code> the first few cells of the spin still
    show the old mix. Set pools before you call <code>spin()</code>.
  </Callout>

  <h2>OffsetCalculator is dead code</h2>

  <p>
    If you open <code>frame/OffsetCalculator.ts</code> you will find a class
    that computes a cross-axis offset per <code>[reel][cell]</code>, either <code>'none'</code> or a <code>'trapezoid'</code> interpolated from <code>startFactor</code> to <code>endFactor</code>. It is not exported,
    nothing in the package constructs it, and <code>builder.offsetConfig()</code> stores a value that nothing reads. It plays no part in big symbols,
    per-reel geometry or curvature; those are handled by <code>Reel._blockSize</code> and <code>_finalizeFrame</code>, <code>mainOffset</code> and <code>reelExtents</code>, and <code>ReelCurve</code> respectively. Do not design against it.
  </p>

